{"id":"e789412f-fd93-4f8f-b9de-b5bc73577249","object":"chat.completion","created":1785005864,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    req_syms = case.get('required_symbols')\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(req_syms, list):\\n        raise ValueError('Invalid payload')\\n\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n.get('id')\\n        if not nid or not isinstance(nid, str):\\n            raise ValueError('Invalid node id')\\n        parent = n.get('parent')\\n        depth = n.get('depth')\\n        removable = n.get('removable')\\n        symbol = n.get('symbol')\\n        if depth is None or removable is None or symbol is None:\\n            raise ValueError('Missing fields in node')\\n        if not isinstance(depth, int) or not isinstance(removable, bool) or not isinstance(symbol, str):\\n            raise ValueError('Field types incorrect')\\n        node_map[nid] = {'id': nid, 'parent': parent, 'depth': depth, 'removable': removable, 'symbol': symbol}\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n\\n    root_nodes = [nid for nid, n in node_map.items() if n['parent'] is None]\\n    if len(root_nodes) != 1:\\n        raise ValueError('Invalid tree: must have exactly one root')\\n    root_id = root_nodes[0]\\n\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    sym_count = {}\\n    for nid, n in node_map.items():\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        sym_count[sym] = sym_count.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n\\n    for sym in req_syms:\\n        if sym not in sym_count or sym_count[sym] == 0:\\n            raise ValueError('Required symbol not present in tree')\\n\\n    candidate_nodes = sorted(node_map.values(), key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\\n\\n    removed = []\\n    for n in candidate_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        children = children_map.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children):\\n            continue\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        new_count = sym_count[sym] - 1\\n        ok = True\\n        for rsym in req_syms:\\n            if rsym == sym and new_count == 0:\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if not ok:\\n            continue\\n        sym_count[sym] = new_count\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed, 'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` for task SE-PE-01. The case payload contains nodes (list of objects with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols (list of symbols). The goal: grammar-guided node reduction, likely similar to Perses algorithm: remove nodes that are removable, while preserving required symbols and grammar validity? But the description is vague. Let's parse carefully.\n\nThe task is registered as \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\". The mechanism is \"Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar; parser generation and real compiler execution are excluded.\" So we have a tree of nodes with parent-child relationships based on \"parent\" field (string id of parent). The root has parent null. Each node has a symbol, depth, removable flag, utility. The goal is to remove some nodes (those with removable=True?) but not others. We need to output `removed_node_ids` (ordered list of IDs of nodes removed, in the order they were removed) and `retained_node_ids` (sorted list of IDs of nodes that remain after reduction).\n\nThe description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we must ensure that after removal, the set of symbols present in the remaining nodes includes all required_symbols. Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we cannot remove a node that has children that are retained. That means we can only remove leaf nodes (or nodes whose descendants are all removed)?? Actually \"retained children\" means if a child node is retained, then the parent cannot be removed. So effectively we remove nodes from leaves upward: we can remove a node only if it has no retained children (i.e., all its descendants are either removed or not present? Actually children that are not removed are retained. So we must remove children before parents.\n\nAlso \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process nodes in order of descending depth (deepest first). If same depth, use node ID as tie break (stable). That defines candidate order.\n\n\"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Before removing a node, check if after removal, all required symbols are still present in the tree (considering nodes that are not removed yet). Additionally, \"removable\" flag: only nodes with removable=True can be removed.\n\nUtility is given but not used in the description? Possibly it's for tie-breaking or scoring, but the instructions say use node ID as tie break at equal depth. So utility is ignored.\n\nThe output: `removed_node_ids` is list of IDs in the order they were removed. `retained_node_ids` is sorted list of IDs of nodes that are not removed (including root, required symbols, non-removable nodes, and removable nodes that were not removed because they fail conditions).\n\nWe also need to validate payload: nodes list must have id, parent, symbol, depth, removable (bool), utility (float). required_symbols list. The root must exist and parent null. Ensure no cycles? Probably assume valid.\n\nWe need to build a tree structure from flat list. Use dictionary mapping id->node object. Also build adjacency children list.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Parse input: nodes list, required_symbols set.\n2. Build node_map: id -> dict with all fields.\n3. Build children_map: parent_id -> list of child ids. Also root = node with parent None.\n4. Validate: root exists, all required fields present, etc. Raise ValueError if not.\n5. Determine initial retained set: all node ids. But we will decide removal.\n6. We need to process candidates in order: depth descending, then by id lexicographically? Use node ID string as tie-break. So we sort nodes by (-depth, id).\n7. For each candidate node in that order:\n   - Check if node is removable and not root (root not removable anyway, but also check removable flag).\n   - Check if node's removable is True.\n   - Check if node has any retained children? Actually we need to check if node has any children that are still retained (not yet removed). Since we process deeper first, children may have been removed already. So we need to query current retained set. That is, if the node has any child id that is still in retained set, then cannot remove.\n   - Check if removing this node would cause any required symbol to be missing. That means: after removal, for every symbol in required_symbols, there must be at least one node in retained set (excluding this node) that has that symbol. So we need to check symbol coverage. We can maintain a count of nodes per symbol among retained nodes. Initially count symbols. When we tentatively remove a node, we decrement count for its symbol. If any count becomes zero for a required symbol, then removal is invalid. So we can keep a dict symbol->count of retained nodes. But careful: multiple nodes can have the same symbol. So if there is another node with same symbol, it's okay. We maintain counts.\n\n   - If all checks pass, we remove the node: add to removed list, remove from retained set, update symbol counts.\n\n8. After processing all candidates, we have `removed_node_ids` in removal order, and `retained_node_ids` is sorted list of remaining IDs (sorted lexicographically maybe? The spec says \"sorted retained IDs\". It doesn't specify sorting order but likely ascending string order for consistent output. In example output format, both arrays are arrays of strings. So we can sort retained node IDs lexicographically.\n\n9. Return JSON: {\"removed_node_ids\": list, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(list)}.\n\nEdge cases: Raise ValueError if input validation fails (e.g., missing fields, cycles, root not found). Also if required_symbols contains a symbol not present in any node, then it's impossible to retain; we should raise ValueError.\n\nAlso note: The description says \"Do not remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" But a node might have symbol that is required (i.e., its symbol is in required_symbols). That does not mean the node itself is required; it's the symbol that is required. So we cannot remove a node if it is the only node with that required symbol? Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That could be interpreted as: if the node's symbol is required, we cannot remove it regardless of whether other nodes have that symbol. But that seems too strict. The earlier \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" indicates that we only need to ensure the symbol is present after removal, not that the specific node cannot be removed. So I think \"a required symbol\" means a node whose symbol is required cannot be removed only if it would make the symbol absent? But the phrasing \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" might be ambiguous. However, later it says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That suggests we can remove a node even if its symbol is required as long as another node with that symbol remains. So \"a required symbol\" likely refers to the root and nodes that are themselves marked as required? But there is no such field. The only \"required\" is required_symbols list. I think the safer interpretation is: nodes with `removable=False` are non-removable. But root has removable=False. Required symbols are not node-specific. So we should only check the removable flag and the conditions. The \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" might be a misinterpretation? Let's re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It might be listing three separate things: (1) root (which is non-removable anyway), (2) a node whose symbol is in required_symbols, (3) node with retained children. But that would make many nodes unremovable. In the fixture examples, nodes n1 has symbol s1 which is required, and its removable=False, so it's fine. Node n4 also has symbol s1 but removable=True and it's a candidate for removal. In the fixture outputs? We don't have expected outputs for these cases, but presumably n4 should be considered for removal. If we interpret \"required symbol\" as any node with a required symbol, then n4 cannot be removed. But that seems too restrictive and would make reduction trivial. The algorithm described (Perses) typically removes nodes that are \"removable\" (i.e., can be deleted) while preserving the \"interestingness\" (here reduced to preserving required symbols). So I think the correct interpretation is: we cannot remove a node if it would cause any required symbol to be missing. So we need to ensure symbol coverage. Therefore the condition \"required symbol\" in the \"Never remove\" list is likely a misphrasing and should be interpreted as \"Never remove a node if it is the only node providing a required symbol\". But the statement says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It explicitly includes \"a required symbol\". Maybe it means a node whose symbol is required? That would contradict the later condition. Let's check the source: The candidate_artifact says: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Then \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" These two are contradictory if \"required symbol\" means node with required symbol. Because if a node is a required symbol, then removing it would violate \"never remove a required symbol\" even if symbol remains represented. So perhaps \"required symbol\" here refers to nodes that are marked as required in the input? But there is no such field. Only nodes have removable flag. The root is non-removable (removable=False). Maybe they mean \"a node that is required for the grammar\" i.e., the root and any node whose symbol is required? The grammar might define that certain symbols are non-removable. But the payload doesn't have that. I think the safer interpretation is to follow the explicit algorithm:  \n- Nodes with removable=False cannot be removed.  \n- Root is removable=False, so it's covered.  \n- Nodes with retained children cannot be removed.  \n- Only remove if after removal all required symbols are still present (via any remaining node).  \n\nAdditionally, the condition \"Never remove a required symbol\" might be a redundancy: a required symbol node cannot be removed if it's the only one; but if there are multiple nodes with that symbol, we could remove one. The phrase \"never remove a required symbol\" could be interpreted as \"never remove a node that is itself a required symbol\" i.e., the node ID is in some required list? Not given. So I'll ignore that specific phrase and rely on the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\". That matches the Perses algorithm where you can remove any node as long as it doesn't break the property.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, but the instructions later emphasize \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved, registered_candidate_order, stable_ties.\" So \"interestingness_preserved\" corresponds to preserving required symbols. \"grammar_valid\" might be about not breaking grammar rules, but here we don't have grammar rules beyond parent-child relationships? Possibly we need to ensure that after removal, the tree is still a valid tree? Removing a node doesn't break tree structure; we just remove the node; its children are already removed first (since we process depth-first). That will be ensured by \"never remove a node with retained children\". So grammar_valid might be implied.\n\nThus implementation:  \n- Build tree and compute initial symbol counts.\n- Sort nodes by depth descending, then by id (lexicographic).\n- Iterate, for each node: check removable, check no retained children (any child id still in retained set), check symbol count after removal for required symbols: if for any required symbol, count after decrement becomes 0, skip.\n- If all pass, remove: add to removed list, remove from retained set, decrement symbol count.\n\nReturn result.\n\nPotential issues: Depth might be zero for root, but other nodes may have same depth? Probably not, as depth increases down. But use negative depth for sorting.\n\nAlso need to handle missing fields: raise ValueError.\n\nNow produce Python code as JSON string.\n\nMake sure to use only standard library, no imports beyond json, sys? But json is allowed for serialization? The function must return a JSON-serializable object; we can just use Python dicts/lists. No need to import json in solve. We'll just return dict.\n\nEdge: The payload may have \"_case_nonce\" field, ignore.\n\nImplementation steps in code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # case is dict with keys: nodes, required_symbols\n    nodes = case.get(\"nodes\")\n    req_syms = case.get(\"required_symbols\")\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(req_syms, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid payload\")\n    \n    # Build map and children\n    node_map = {}\n    children_map = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        nid = n.get(\"id\")\n        if not nid or not isinstance(nid, str):\n            raise ValueError(\"Invalid node id\")\n        parent = n.get(\"parent\")\n        depth = n.get(\"depth\")\n        removable = n.get(\"removable\")\n        symbol = n.get(\"symbol\")\n        utility = n.get(\"utility\")  # not used but validate?\n        if depth is None or removable is None or symbol is None:\n            raise ValueError(\"Missing fields in node\")\n        if not isinstance(depth, int) or not isinstance(removable, bool) or not isinstance(symbol, str):\n            raise ValueError(\"Field types incorrect\")\n        node_map[nid] = {\"id\": nid, \"parent\": parent, \"depth\": depth, \"removable\": removable, \"symbol\": symbol}\n        # children\n        if parent is not None:\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\n        else:\n            # root\n            pass\n    \n    # Find root\n    root_nodes = [nid for nid, n in node_map.items() if n[\"parent\"] is None]\n    if len(root_nodes) != 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid tree: must have exactly one root\")\n    root_id = root_nodes[0]\n    \n    # Validate no cycles? Assume valid.\n    \n    # Initial retained set and symbol counts\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    sym_count = {}\n    for nid, n in node_map.items():\n        sym = n[\"symbol\"]\n        sym_count[sym] = sym_count.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    \n    # Check that all required symbols exist initially\n    for sym in req_syms:\n        if sym not in sym_count or sym_count[sym] == 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Required symbol not present in tree\")\n    \n    # Order candidates: depth desc, id asc\n    candidate_nodes = sorted(node_map.values(), key=lambda x: (-x[\"depth\"], x[\"id\"]))\n    \n    removed = []\n    for n in candidate_nodes:\n        nid = n[\"id\"]\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue # already removed\n        if not n[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue # root is not removable anyway but safe\n        # check no retained children\n        children = children_map.get(nid, [])\n        if any(child in retained for child in children):\n            continue\n        # tentative removal: check symbol coverage\n        sym = n[\"symbol\"]\n        # if after removal, any required symbol count becomes zero, skip\n        new_count = sym_count[sym] - 1\n        ok = True\n        for rsym in req_syms:\n            if rsym == sym:\n                if new_count == 0:\n                    ok = False\n                    break\n            else:\n                # other symbols unaffected\n                pass\n        if not ok:\n            continue\n        # remove\n        sym_count[sym] = new_count\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removed.append(nid)\n    \n    # retained set now has remaining nodes; sort\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nBut we must ensure we don't mutate case - we only read.\n\nAlso note: The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The removal order is already in the order we processed (depth desc). That seems fine.\n\nNow we need to produce the final JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a JSON string.\n\nWe must escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nLet's write the code as a string. We'll generate it carefully.\n\nWe also need to include docstring? Not required but fine.\n\nI'll produce a clean version."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":4249,"total_tokens":6418,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3562},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}